{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["And the reason for <b>כִּי גִלָּה כְּנַף אָבִיו</b> (\"for he uncovered his father's wing\") is a matter of honor toward the father. For in the Torah He mentioned (Leviticus 20:11) עֶרְוַת אָבִיו גִּלָּה (\"he uncovered his father's nakedness\"), and likewise He mentioned regarding his brother's wife and his father's brother's wife their עֶרְוָה (\"nakedness\"), because there He holds them liable for a severe punishment, death or karet, and it was necessary to speak disgracefully of the evil they committed — therefore He mentioned עֶרְוָה with regard to them. But here, since it is a matter of curse, it is sufficient to mention כִּי גִלָּה כְּנַף אָבִיו, for by the uncovering of the father's wing alone one is liable to a curse, because he debases his honor, and whoever debases his father is cursed. And likewise, לֹא יִקַּח אִישׁ אֶת אֵשֶׁת אָבִיו וְלֹא יְגַלֶּה כְּנַף אָבִיו (Deuteronomy 23:1) — this too is a matter of honor: that he shall not draw near to her, for it is not fitting to uncover the wing that he spread over a woman by way of marriage, in the manner of וּפָרַשְׂתָּ כְנָפֶךָ עַל אֲמָתְךָ (Ruth 3:9), and not like the one who expounds it as אֲנוּסַת אָבִיו (Yevamot 97a)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "The reference to Yevamot 97a records the page; the sugya discussing אֲנוּסַת אָבִיו spans nearby pages in that perek."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:56:11.946842700Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}